  {
   "where": "main/2",
   "type": "title",
   "value": "🔋 Understand Batteries with the Tank Analogy",
   "help": "",
   "key": null,
   "proto": {
    "tag": "h1",
    "body": "🔋 Understand Batteries with the Tank Analogy"
   }
  },
  {
   "where": "main/3",
   "block": "expander",
   "proto": {
    "label": "🧠 What does this tool show?",
    "expanded": false
   }
  },
  {
   "where": "main/3/0",
   "type": "markdown",
   "value": "Think of a battery like **two water tanks** that share the same water.\n\n            - One tank is the **anode**, the other is the **cathode**.  \n            - The **water** is like the lithium that moves back and forth.  \n\n            💧 When you **charge** the battery, you **pump water uphill** into the anode tank.  \n            ⚡ When you **use** the battery, the water flows **downhill** back to the cathode.\n\n            The **height difference** between the two tanks is the **voltage** of the battery.  \n            That's how much “push” the battery can give.  \n\n            Each tank has its own **shape**. Some parts are wide (can hold a lot of water), some are narrow (hold less).  \n            This shape is shown in the **dQ/dV plot**.  \n\n            If we keep track of the height difference as the tanks fill and empty, we get the **OCV curve** — the battery’s special fingerprint.\n\n            ---\n\n            ### ⚙️ The knobs you can play with:\n            - **SoC** → how full the tanks are right now  \n            - **Vmin & Vmax** → the lowest and highest “water levels” we allow  \n            - **NP ratio** → how big one tank is compared to the other  \n            - **LiP ratio** → how much total water (lithium) is in the system  \n\n            👉 Turn the knobs and watch how the tanks and the voltage curve change!",
   "help": "",
   "key": null,
   "proto": {
    "body": "Think of a battery like **two water tanks** that share the same water.\n\n            - One tank is the **anode**, the other is the **cathode**.  \n            - The **water** is like the lithium that moves back and forth.  \n\n            💧 When you **charge** the battery, you **pump water uphill** into the anode tank.  \n            ⚡ When you **use** the battery, the water flows **downhill** back to the cathode.\n\n            The **height difference** between the two tanks is the **voltage** of the battery.  \n            That's how much “push” the battery can give.  \n\n            Each tank has its own **shape**. Some parts are wide (can hold a lot of water), some are narrow (hold less).  \n            This shape is shown in the **dQ/dV plot**.  \n\n            If we keep track of the height difference as the tanks fill and empty, we get the **OCV curve** — the battery’s special fingerprint.\n\n            ---\n\n            ### ⚙️ The knobs you can play with:\n            - **SoC** → how full the tanks are right now  \n            - **Vmin & Vmax** → the lowest and highest “water levels” we allow  \n            - **NP ratio** → how big one tank is compared to the other  \n            - **LiP ratio** → how much total water (lithium) is in the system  \n\n            👉 Turn the knobs and watch how the tanks and the voltage curve change!",
    "element_type": "NATIVE"
   }
  },
